{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED exact clean pushed head 35c8ed81c143b8409fb44d70e67a5474fc41ba96 against artifact ffe61bbe, parent request d6e869e9, child request 362c01e0, and the earlier ratified changes-requested verdict.\n\nArchitecture: this head changes only the kernel checkpoint/cache implementation and its tests. It preserves the layer contract in docs/reference/architecture.md: the cache still contains only kernel-verified opaque event material, compact checkpoint schema v3 and streamed verification behavior are unchanged, and the documented 256 MiB serialized ceiling remains available because finished-artifact limiting is separate from construction-peak limiting. No architecture-page update is required.\n\nSecurity: I traced untrusted payloads and attachments through append, tail growth, owned-tail flush, borrowed chunk construction, failure, marshal, and replay. Exact-capacity clones make admission match retained payload capacity; the model explicitly distinguishes exact measurements, the conservative tail-array cover, the attachment-map estimate, and excluded constant-size/allocator effects. The linked chunk list removes slice reallocation; node storage, retained chunk capacity, tail backing storage, the old array during growth, the in-hand entry during compression, output capacity, and its growth-copy headroom are charged before allocation or retained until release. Overflow fails the checkpoint closed while the verified append/read continues, and failed-cache writers reserve the whole budget. Marshal now rejects either direction of explicit chunk-count divergence before trusting it for the event-count identity. Signatures, secrets, authority, and the 256 MiB read bound are unchanged. I found no unresolved security defect.\n\nSimplification: the linked list and the separate checkpointLimitWriter/checkpointChunkWriter are necessary distinctions: a slice recreates the unbounded growth transient, while one writer policy would conflate the full serialized